WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.470
The deep seafloor contains ridges, abyssal plains, seamounts and trenches.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:09.850
An abyssal plain is relatively smooth because sediment buries much of

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:13.810
the original roughness. Seamounts rise above that plain, often as submarine

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:18.090
volcanoes. Trenches are narrow, very deep bends where an oceanic plate

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.270
descends beneath another plate. They occupy a small area despite their

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:23.170
exceptional depths.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:28.070
New lithosphere is hot and relatively buoyant near a spreading center.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:31.870
As it moves away, cooling increases its density and the seafloor

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:35.610
subsides. That explains the broad ridge-to-basin elevation trend.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:40.570
Continental shelves are shallower still, whereas subduction trenches can be far

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:44.390
deeper. Local features also matter, so depth alone is not an

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.490
exact clock for every point on a map.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:51.630
Compare the sediment-thickness globes. Older crust has had more time to

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:56.050
collect sediment, but nearby land and major rivers strongly affect supply.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.630
Thick deposits line many continental margins and river mouths.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:03.850
The youngest ridge crust has little cover, and remote ocean areas

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:08.290
can remain thinly covered. A thick deposit therefore records both available

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:11.250
time and sediment delivery, not age alone.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.070
The age maps use their own color key.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.050
Young crust traces spreading centers;

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:19.310
progressively older crust generally lies farther away.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.010
The supplied Atlantic map has paired age bands on opposite sides

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.950
of the ridge. Its oldest surviving colors are a property of

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:30.430
this particular map and source, not an unqualified universal maximum age

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:31.750
for every oceanic rock.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:37.340
The Pacific pattern is less symmetrical because subduction removes oceanic lithosphere.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.540
Some young crust approaches the American margins while older, deeper seafloor

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.640
survives elsewhere, especially toward the western Pacific.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.960
Compare depth, age and sediment maps side by side.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.200
State what each map measures, identify the spatial pattern, then explain

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.220
how creation, cooling, burial and destruction produce it.
